  <header>
    <h1>China Transit Visa & 240-Hour Visa-Free Transit Guide</h1>
    <p>Last verified: August 28, 2026</p>
  </header>

  <section>
    <h2>Quick Answer</h2>

    <p>
      China's 240-hour visa-free transit policy allows eligible travelers to enter
      Mainland China without obtaining a regular Chinese visa when they are
      transiting through China to a different country or region.
    </p>

    <p>
      As of August 2026, the policy covers nationals of 57 countries, including
      the United States, and applies through 65 designated ports in 24
      provincial-level regions. Eligible travelers may remain within the
      designated permitted areas for no more than 240 hours, subject to the
      policy requirements.
    </p>

    <p><strong>The most important route rule is:</strong></p>

    <p>
      The country or region immediately before Mainland China and the country or
      region immediately after Mainland China must be different.
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Los Angeles &rarr; Shanghai &rarr; Tokyo:</strong> passes the transit-route
        test because the relevant route is United States &rarr; Mainland China &rarr; Japan.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Los Angeles &rarr; Shanghai &rarr; San Francisco:</strong> does not pass the
        transit-route test because the relevant route is United States &rarr; Mainland
        China &rarr; United States.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      A route that passes this test is not automatically eligible for 240-hour
      visa-free transit. Your nationality, passport/travel document, onward
      travel, entry and exit ports, length of stay, permitted travel areas and
      other requirements must also qualify. Immigration authorities make the
      final decision during entry inspection.
    </p>
  </section>
